          ><div class="i1s8jSyqnxESWb4yG" data-instant-type="container"
            ><div class="ie10r2B3TjprieDNt" data-instant-type="container"
              ><div class="i65HmZ3flvKEgHpwM" data-instant-type="container"
                ><div data-instant-type="text" class="instant-rich-text ilwmd6UrGRhivUV1c"
                  ><p><strong>The power of autopilot</strong></p></div
                ><div data-instant-type="text" class="instant-rich-text ielfEiXlrNQB0aJgo"><p>A habit is behavior that through regular repetition is automatically executed in response to environmental cues. This response becomes deeply embedded in the brain, requiring little to no conscious decision-making. This is evolutionarily efficient: it saves energy.</p><p></p><p>Behavioral scientists like Wendy Wood have extensively researched how these routines develop. Their research shows that approximately 40% of our daily actions occur completely automatically. We don&apos;t think about it, we just do it because the context prompts us to.</p></div></div
              ></div
            ></div
          ></div
        ><div class="iDHZqzY4CVMGD8ez4" data-instant-type="container"
          ><div data-instant-type="image" class="i0wm8bBWRBFIrUEh5"><img alt="" sizes="(max-width: 1280px) 100vw, 1280px" srcSet="https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/VnWLpuc0ZVCyx5ix/screenshot-2025-12-26-at-104636pm.webp?width=1080 1080w, https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/VnWLpuc0ZVCyx5ix/screenshot-2025-12-26-at-104636pm.webp?width=828 828w, https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/VnWLpuc0ZVCyx5ix/screenshot-2025-12-26-at-104636pm.webp?width=750 750w, https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/VnWLpuc0ZVCyx5ix/screenshot-2025-12-26-at-104636pm.webp?width=640 640w, https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/VnWLpuc0ZVCyx5ix/screenshot-2025-12-26-at-104636pm.webp?width=360 360w, https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/VnWLpuc0ZVCyx5ix/screenshot-2025-12-26-at-104636pm.webp?width=1280 1280w" src="https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/VnWLpuc0ZVCyx5ix/screenshot-2025-12-26-at-104636pm.webp?width=1280" width="880" height="834" decoding="async" loading="eager" fetchpriority="auto" class="instant-image instant-image__main"></div
          ><div class="iRkHIUvPYMUSHJqEE" data-instant-type="container"
            ><div class="iChDKenEnpEEbkm2e" data-instant-type="container"
              ><div class="iXxolkCE2JoXYItAr" data-instant-type="container"
                ><div data-instant-type="text" class="instant-rich-text i5dEZSJH5IhvjHv1Y"><p>The anatomy of a habit: The &apos;Habit Loop&apos;</p></div
                ><div data-instant-type="text" class="instant-rich-text i4yWhF1YHcOclE7pc"
                  ><p>To understand (and change) habits, scientists often use the &apos;Habit Loop&apos; framework. This consists of four inseparable components:</p><p></p><p><strong>Context Cue</strong>: A signal or situation that triggers the behavior (for example: waiting for the bus, stress at work, or a cup of coffee).</p><p><strong>Craving</strong>: The motivation behind the behavior; the urge to change the feeling from the trigger.</p><p><strong>Routine</strong>: The actual action or ritual itself.</p><p><strong>Reward</strong>: The result that reinforces the behavior in the brain and completes the circle.</p></div
                ></div
              ></div
            ></div
          ></div
        ><div class="iges9HCgS4WsvtEpT" data-instant-type="container"
