t-2025-12-26-at-110220pm.webp?width=1280 1280w" src="https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/qfRyhwYUEuoLDUlr/screenshot-2025-12-26-at-110220pm.webp?width=1280" width="820" height="832" decoding="async" loading="eager" fetchpriority="auto" class="instant-image instant-image__main instant-responsive instant-responsive--mobile"></div
          ><div class="iaa5MkuDTvh9PZAp9" data-instant-type="container"
            ><div class="i9Su71ZsGqzf7rfN9" data-instant-type="container"
              ><div class="iFt2fJURibZX8SqTa" data-instant-type="container"
                ><div data-instant-type="text" class="instant-rich-text iYje8ecA55a3gEBOQ"><p>Why bad habits are so deeply rooted</p></div
                ><div data-instant-type="text" class="instant-rich-text iNzJDDfzhZoWz5a9M"><p>Research shows that bad habits are extremely strongly connected to these automatic &apos;loops&apos;. Even when someone consciously decides to quit, the Context Cues (the triggers) remain active in the brain. In studies on addiction, the degree of automatism directly correlates with the intensity of the behavior. This means that the action of your bad habit - holding it, bringing it to your mouth, inhaling - is a deeply ingrained habit component that is separate from nicotine addiction.</p></div></div
              ></div
            ></div
          ></div
        ><div class="iAu1hjKTrSRybhgW9" data-instant-type="container"
          ><div data-instant-type="image" class="ikVO4mZgPnsRI6TJW"><img alt="" sizes="(max-width: 1280px) 100vw, 1280px" srcSet="https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/0IFtZmACrL4gNIqW/screenshot-2025-12-22-at-72740pm.webp?width=1080 1080w, https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/0IFtZmACrL4gNIqW/screenshot-2025-12-22-at-72740pm.webp?width=828 828w, https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/0IFtZmACrL4gNIqW/screenshot-2025-12-22-at-72740pm.webp?width=750 750w, https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/0IFtZmACrL4gNIqW/screenshot-2025-12-22-at-72740pm.webp?width=640 640w, https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/0IFtZmACrL4gNIqW/screenshot-2025-12-22-at-72740pm.webp?width=360 360w, https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/0IFtZmACrL4gNIqW/screenshot-2025-12-22-at-72740pm.webp?width=1280 1280w" src="https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/0IFtZmACrL4gNIqW/screenshot-2025-12-22-at-72740pm.webp?width=1280" width="610" height="586" decoding="async" loading="eager" fetchpriority="auto" class="instant-image instant-image__main instant-responsive instant-responsive--desktop instant-responsive--laptop instant-responsive--tablet"><img alt="" sizes="(max-width: 1280px) 100vw, 1280px" srcSet="https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/VSp5UK2UWdQlo7mH/screenshot-2025-12-22-at-72831pm.webp?width=1080 1080w, https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/VSp5UK2UWdQlo7mH/screenshot-2025-12-22-at-72831pm.webp?width=828 828w, https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/VSp5UK2UWdQlo7mH/screenshot-2025-12-22-at-72831pm.webp?width=750 750w, https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/VSp5UK2UWdQlo7mH/screenshot-2025-12-22-at-72831pm.webp?width=640 640w, https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/VSp5UK2UWdQlo7mH/screenshot-2025-12-22-at-72831pm.webp?width=360 360w, https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/VSp5UK2UWdQlo7mH/screenshot-2025-12-22-at-72831pm.webp?width=1280 1280w" src="https://cdn.instant.so/sites/1ATWZ62dPhRUmsuT/assets/VSp5UK2UWdQlo7mH/screenshot-2025-12-22-at-72831pm.webp?width=1280" width="568" height="584" decoding="async" loading="eager" fetchpriority="auto" class="instant-image instant-image__main instant-responsive instant-responsive--mobile"></div
          ><div class="ihKg8mHIFMnGy8twS" data-instant-type="container"
            ><div class="i2vAyWOh9XtLqN80n" data-instant-type="container"
              ><div class="ivPDrxps1U1kBBIFo" data-instant-type="container"
                ><div data-instant-type="text" class="instant-rich-text izCBk0Dpd48eqFGqn"><p>Change is a process, </p><p>not a switch</p></div
                ><div data-instant-type="text" class="instant-rich-text iS79h0qtv9EyOLOev"
                  ><p>Behavioral change, like quitting a bad habit, is rarely a linear process. The Transtheoretical Model (TTM) is an influential model that describes how people move through different stages:</p><p></p><p><strong>Awareness</strong>: Realizing that the behavior exists.</p><p><strong>Contemplation</strong>: Thinking about change.</p><p><strong>Planning</strong>: Preparing how to approach the change.</p><p><strong>Action</strong>: The actual attempt to change.</p><p><strong>Maintenance</strong>: Integrating new behavior. </p><p></p><p>This model emphasizes that successful change is an interplay of motivation, the right context, repetition, and confidence that you can do it.</p></div
                ></div
              ></div
            ></div
          ></div
        ><div class="ix3ZpiOwXqwGk268M" data-instant-type="container"
